The row number decides the letter, and that letter is printed as many times as the row number. For example, row 1 prints `A`, row 2 prints `B B`, and row 3 prints `C C C`.

This version uses simple nested loops. It changes only the output stream and does not modify any input.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern16(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            char letter = 'A' + row;

            for (int column = 0; column <= row; column++) {
                cout << letter;
                if (column < row) {
                    cout << ' ';
                }
            }
            cout << '\n';
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 3`, the rows are `A`, `B B`, and `C C C`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`, because the total number of printed letters is `1 + 2 + ... + n`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

A row can be described by two values: its letter and its length. The row index gives both directly. The first row uses `A` and length 1, then the next row uses `B` and length 2.

The code writes spaces only between characters, which keeps the output neat.

```csharp
using System;

class Solution {
    public void pattern16(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            char letter = (char)('A' + row);

            for (int count = 0; count < row + 1; count++) {
                if (count != 0) {
                    Console.Write(' ');
                }
                Console.Write(letter);
            }

            Console.WriteLine();
        }
    }
}
```

An input of `n = 1` produces only `A`. A non-positive value produces no rows, since the outer loop never starts.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` based on the number of characters printed.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output handled by the console.

This uses `while` loops instead of `for` loops. The outer counter selects the letter, and the inner counter prints that letter the required number of times. The cast to `char` changes `0` into `A`, `1` into `B`, and so on.

The array or input is not modified. For `n = 4`, the last row is `D D D D`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    void pattern16(int n) {
        int row = 0;

        while (row < n) {
            char letter = static_cast<char>('A' + row);
            int count = 0;

            while (count <= row) {
                if (count > 0) {
                    cout << ' ';
                }
                cout << letter;
                count++;
            }

            cout << '\n';
            row++;
        }
    }
};
```

The total work is the sum of the row lengths. So for `n` rows, it prints `n * (n + 1) / 2` letters.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

Each line has one repeated character. The character starts at `A` and moves forward after every row. A `StringBuilder` collects one row before printing it, so the spacing stays easy to control.

If `n` is zero, the loops simply print nothing. The input value is not changed.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern16(int n) {
        for (int row = 0; row < n; row++) {
            StringBuilder line = new StringBuilder();
            char letter = (char) ('A' + row);

            for (int column = 0; column <= row; column++) {
                if (column > 0) {
                    line.append(' ');
                }
                line.append(letter);
            }

            System.out.println(line);
        }
    }
}
```

The builder is cleared by creating a new one for each row. This makes the code a little longer than printing directly, but it avoids a trailing space at the end of a line.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` for creating and printing all rows.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the largest row stored in the builder.

